1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,916
Your laptop has a private address, yet it reaches the whole internet. How?

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:06,970
Five ideas make that work,

3
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:10,671
and you will meet them in every cloud and every company network.

4
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:13,868
Routing decides where a packet goes next.

5
00:00:13,868 --> 00:00:16,450
N A T rewrites addresses on the way out.

6
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:18,709
A firewall decides what is allowed.

7
00:00:19,259 --> 00:00:24,230
A load balancer shares the traffic, and a reverse proxy stands in front of your servers.

8
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:29,367
Today you see all five work, live, on a small network built on this computer.

9
00:00:31,417 --> 00:00:34,638
Start with routing. A router joins networks together.

10
00:00:35,188 --> 00:00:36,404
When a packet arrives,

11
00:00:36,404 --> 00:00:38,834
the router looks at the destination address,

12
00:00:38,834 --> 00:00:40,436
and checks its routing table.

13
00:00:40,986 --> 00:00:45,573
The table says which network is reachable through which door, and who the next hop is.

14
00:00:46,123 --> 00:00:51,051
If nothing matches, the packet goes to the default route, the way out to everything else.

15
00:00:51,601 --> 00:00:55,548
No router knows the whole path. Each one only knows the next step.

16
00:00:57,598 --> 00:00:59,707
Let us look at a real router.

17
00:00:59,707 --> 00:01:00,579
In this lab,

18
00:01:00,579 --> 00:01:04,724
the letters R T mean: run the next command on the router.

19
00:01:04,724 --> 00:01:05,961
Type: rt ip route

20
00:01:06,091 --> 00:01:08,251
The router sits on two networks,

21
00:01:08,251 --> 00:01:09,669
ten dot zero dot one,

22
00:01:09,669 --> 00:01:11,357
and ten dot zero dot two.

23
00:01:11,357 --> 00:01:13,451
That is what makes it a router.

24
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:21,617
One setting turns it on. Type: rt sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

25
00:01:21,747 --> 00:01:25,779
A one means forwarding is on: it passes packets between its networks.

26
00:01:26,329 --> 00:01:35,331
Now switch it off. Type: rt sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=0

27
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:43,163
Then try to reach the server. Type: ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2

28
00:01:43,293 --> 00:01:43,867
No reply.

29
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:45,461
One hundred percent loss.

30
00:01:45,461 --> 00:01:47,437
The router received the packet,

31
00:01:47,437 --> 00:01:49,095
but refused to pass it on.

32
00:01:49,645 --> 00:01:58,989
Switch forwarding back on. Type: rt sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

33
00:02:00,619 --> 00:02:03,414
Next, N A T, network address translation.

34
00:02:03,964 --> 00:02:05,083
Private addresses,

35
00:02:05,083 --> 00:02:06,513
like ten dot something,

36
00:02:06,513 --> 00:02:08,876
or one ninety-two dot one sixty-eight,

37
00:02:08,876 --> 00:02:11,238
cannot be used on the public internet.

38
00:02:11,788 --> 00:02:15,478
So the router swaps your private source address for its own public one,

39
00:02:15,478 --> 00:02:16,674
and remembers the swap.

40
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:21,469
When the reply comes back, it swaps the address back, and hands the answer to you.

41
00:02:22,019 --> 00:02:24,590
Many machines can share one public address,

42
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:27,459
and the outside world only ever sees the router.

43
00:02:29,509 --> 00:02:35,966
A small web server here can tell us who is calling.

44
00:02:35,966 --> 00:02:41,157
Type: curl -s http://10.0.2.2:8080/whoami

45
00:02:41,287 --> 00:02:46,450
You are ten dot zero dot one dot two. The server sees my real, private address.

46
00:02:46,999 --> 00:02:55,192
Now turn on N A T on the router. Type: rt iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -j MASQUERADE

47
00:02:55,322 --> 00:03:05,220
Same request again. Type: curl -s http://10.0.2.2:8080/whoami

48
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:09,298
Now the server sees ten dot zero dot two dot one: the router.

49
00:03:09,298 --> 00:03:11,174
My private address is hidden.

50
00:03:11,724 --> 00:03:17,570
Look at the rule. Type: rt iptables -t nat -S POSTROUTING

51
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:23,097
Masquerade: rewrite the source address to the router's own. That one line is home N A T.

52
00:03:25,147 --> 00:03:29,414
Now the firewall. A firewall is a list of rules, checked from the top.

53
00:03:29,964 --> 00:03:34,636
The first rule that matches wins, and that rule says accept, drop, or reject.

54
00:03:35,186 --> 00:03:39,879
Drop throws the packet away in silence. The sender waits, and then times out.

55
00:03:40,429 --> 00:03:44,504
Reject throws it away, and tells the sender. The sender fails at once.

56
00:03:45,054 --> 00:03:49,768
Remember the last lesson? A time out and a refusal point to different problems.

57
00:03:51,818 --> 00:03:55,448
Let us block the web server's port,

58
00:03:55,448 --> 00:03:57,211
with a drop rule.

59
00:03:57,211 --> 00:04:03,018
Type: rt iptables -A FORWARD -p tcp --dport 8080 -j DROP

60
00:04:03,148 --> 00:04:15,095
Now try the page, and give up after two seconds. Type: curl -sS -m 2 http://10.0.2.2:8080

61
00:04:15,225 --> 00:04:19,811
Operation timed out. Silence. That is what a drop looks like from outside.

62
00:04:20,361 --> 00:04:24,884
Remove the rule. Type: rt iptables -F FORWARD

63
00:04:25,014 --> 00:04:27,228
Now the same block,

64
00:04:27,228 --> 00:04:29,093
but with reject.

65
00:04:29,093 --> 00:04:35,851
Type: rt iptables -A FORWARD -p tcp --dport 8080 -j REJECT

66
00:04:35,981 --> 00:04:46,264
And try the page again. Type: curl -sS -m 2 http://10.0.2.2:8080

67
00:04:46,394 --> 00:04:51,514
Failed to connect, straight away. The sender is told no. Same block, different symptom.

68
00:04:52,064 --> 00:04:56,587
List the rules. Type: rt iptables -S FORWARD

69
00:04:56,717 --> 00:05:02,199
The first line is the default policy: accept. Then our rule, with the port, and reject.

70
00:05:04,249 --> 00:05:07,150
Last, the reverse proxy, and the load balancer.

71
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:10,447
A reverse proxy stands in front of your servers.

72
00:05:10,447 --> 00:05:11,535
Clients talk to it,

73
00:05:11,535 --> 00:05:13,652
and it talks to the servers for them.

74
00:05:14,202 --> 00:05:18,853
That hides the servers, and it can add security, caching, and secure connections.

75
00:05:19,403 --> 00:05:23,074
A load balancer spreads the requests across several servers,

76
00:05:23,074 --> 00:05:24,971
so no single one is overloaded.

77
00:05:25,521 --> 00:05:26,675
If a server dies,

78
00:05:26,675 --> 00:05:29,660
a good balancer stops sending traffic to it.

79
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:31,696
Often one tool does both jobs,

80
00:05:31,696 --> 00:05:32,646
like engine X.

81
00:05:34,696 --> 00:05:40,120
Here the proxy listens on ten dot zero dot one dot one.

82
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:42,685
Behind it are two servers.

83
00:05:42,685 --> 00:05:47,517
Type: for i in 1 2 3 4; do curl -s 10.0.1.1; done

84
00:05:47,647 --> 00:05:53,365
Four requests, to one address. Backend A, backend B, A, B. The proxy takes turns.

85
00:05:53,915 --> 00:05:58,139
The client never learns the servers' addresses. It only knows the proxy.

86
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:02,998
Where does the proxy live? Type: rt ss -ltn

87
00:06:03,128 --> 00:06:04,642
Listening on port eighty,

88
00:06:04,642 --> 00:06:06,459
on the router's front address.

89
00:06:06,459 --> 00:06:07,549
That is the proxy,

90
00:06:07,549 --> 00:06:08,760
waiting for clients.

91
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:12,453
Match the symptom to the cause.

92
00:06:13,003 --> 00:06:17,269
Times out, only through the router: forwarding is off, or a route is missing.

93
00:06:17,819 --> 00:06:20,507
Times out on one port only: a drop rule.

94
00:06:21,057 --> 00:06:24,641
Refused at once: a reject rule, or nothing is listening.

95
00:06:25,191 --> 00:06:29,053
Every client shows the router's address in the logs: that is N A T.

96
00:06:29,603 --> 00:06:32,867
Bad gateway: the proxy cannot reach the server behind it.

97
00:06:33,417 --> 00:06:36,681
One server overloaded, one idle: check the balancing.

98
00:06:38,731 --> 00:06:39,990
Four myths to drop.

99
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:46,086
Myth one: N A T is a firewall. It hides addresses, but it does not decide what is allowed.

100
00:06:46,636 --> 00:06:49,108
Myth two: a blocked port is invisible.

101
00:06:49,108 --> 00:06:50,800
A reject announces itself,

102
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:53,207
and a drop still shows as a time out.

103
00:06:53,757 --> 00:06:58,536
Myth three: a load balancer only does round robin. There are many strategies.

104
00:06:59,086 --> 00:07:04,696
Myth four: a reverse proxy is a proxy for users. It serves the servers, not the users.

105
00:07:05,246 --> 00:07:07,166
Name the job, then pick the tool.

106
00:07:09,216 --> 00:07:11,541
Here is how you check your own understanding.

107
00:07:12,091 --> 00:07:15,185
Explain what a routing table and a default route do.

108
00:07:15,735 --> 00:07:18,231
Explain N A T, and what the server sees.

109
00:07:18,781 --> 00:07:22,941
Explain accept, drop, and reject, and how each looks from outside.

110
00:07:23,491 --> 00:07:25,496
Explain what a load balancer does.

111
00:07:26,046 --> 00:07:29,843
Explain a reverse proxy, and how it differs from a load balancer.

112
00:07:30,393 --> 00:07:34,063
Match a symptom to routing, N A T, firewall, or proxy.

113
00:07:34,613 --> 00:07:36,234
To pass, you need all six.

114
00:07:38,284 --> 00:07:40,567
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

115
00:07:41,117 --> 00:07:44,146
One: run ip route, and find the default route.

116
00:07:44,696 --> 00:07:51,224
Two: run sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward. Is your machine a router?

117
00:07:51,774 --> 00:07:56,958
Three: run sudo iptables dash S, and read the rules you already have.

118
00:07:57,508 --> 00:08:00,676
Four: run curl dash s i f config dot me,

119
00:08:00,676 --> 00:08:03,528
and compare it with ip dash br addr.

120
00:08:03,528 --> 00:08:04,953
That gap is N A T.

121
00:08:05,503 --> 00:08:09,237
Five: for each symptom in the table, write the cause in one line.

122
00:08:09,787 --> 00:08:13,093
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

123
00:08:28,643 --> 00:08:29,518
Quick check.

124
00:08:30,068 --> 00:08:31,668
What does the default route do?

125
00:08:32,218 --> 00:08:33,604
What does N A T change?

126
00:08:34,154 --> 00:08:36,053
How do drop and reject differ?

127
00:08:36,603 --> 00:08:39,824
Load balancer or reverse proxy: what is the difference?

128
00:08:40,374 --> 00:08:44,492
Objective covered: MC03-O06.

129
00:08:45,042 --> 00:08:47,111
